#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7ed59c4-7f8d-511d-b2cd-912a8ca1bbd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364573} {:answer/id #uuid "e36a7df3-4b60-51eb-8410-ea9f59627a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364574} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6c8134-625e-517b-b13e-9abb15edb487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364575} {:answer/id #uuid "e58864ec-5f87-5414-bdde-7a3d7aca3efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364576} {:answer/id #uuid "56d8cf97-4f74-56a0-aa68-2555b3185d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e58864ec-5f87-5414-bdde-7a3d7aca3efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364576} :answer-field/id #uuid "35f9ac41-4f96-571a-8018-1cc0d2bb0983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "949bd398-6573-53d2-bf2d-21cf0dde3f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-142000" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/98edb404419e7ab515109f87addb44709513007b1a0105b648a3ce2b62ad547f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+1=4.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b94c36f6-5a86-5bea-a203-cacfb66da3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364580} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb3db5c-e6b9-59ca-a168-2117861b71e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364581} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca5e747-b893-52e8-a886-2ad43b181501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364582} {:answer/id #uuid "eb51bc2d-ca9a-54b1-beaa-611941e8a47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364583} {:answer/id #uuid "e29bd491-edc3-5252-a7e1-b44d9ab15add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb3db5c-e6b9-59ca-a168-2117861b71e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364581} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6d8be10-97c4-58fe-8896-2f77d8ac46b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e775babe-aff7-50d7-903d-d93ed0c9bdc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143668" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/f6ee30d5c189c9579a1f38d59f06581a7cfff944d42489eab2e3c0c6ce089ad4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{12}{5}$ units down. Hence, $D=-\\frac{12}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{5}+(-\\frac{12}{5})=-\\frac{9}{5}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f00686a-47d8-510d-9126-f8a1842eb2bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364587} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdc2494-06db-5e3c-8680-e17dc3e997e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364588} {:answer/id #uuid "9e46b978-291c-5371-8332-f8ac43b31238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364589} {:answer/id #uuid "39daa270-eb68-5ba9-84a4-cd6aee1b7ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364590} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0afeb4-d0c8-524d-ab14-f1b98191d12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e46b978-291c-5371-8332-f8ac43b31238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364589} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4476e94-74ca-573c-b4d8-ca005305943b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb50bb2b-5c10-590e-b034-9707381ca9b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143674" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/ee4ad78545160a052a28f0f0fcc7df476cc2063e48429a315a9cb9242f7d5311.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/e1ed3a322c82b5c70acb01c54c97b09814f41aec80d980034a6fcdc46037209c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8a48ad-7fbc-5d7d-b628-66506c916a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364594} {:answer/id #uuid "feb40eb3-62fe-52dd-8a15-caddede53097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364595} {:answer/id #uuid "44f1d945-325e-5235-a7da-c40e8995a9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364596} {:answer/id #uuid "05ba1499-ac25-5d08-b8ba-ed30e10d71fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364597} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6368ee-90e3-5cb1-8d37-197ba61c328c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb40eb3-62fe-52dd-8a15-caddede53097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364595} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf59e94-c44c-520b-a6ea-c9da07142fd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad1e1c5f-8601-5df4-a3fc-7ce84f2d264b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65571" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/2f11d5b11c53f7b826291e0b7e95828f57d6f1242b768baf12c874be229b2935.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(\\frac{3}{5})\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4228c9e-2daa-5e11-a55e-1c5a92843977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183618} {:answer/id #uuid "fb45ecdb-eb87-50e4-80fb-f9107637e101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183619} {:answer/id #uuid "245a1149-13f8-5308-b0b2-206e74a9f960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183620} {:answer/id #uuid "438afc9e-1859-58e4-b9cc-f6a30f6cce18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183621} {:answer/id #uuid "511294c2-7ee2-5a2a-bba5-b44681efb733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4228c9e-2daa-5e11-a55e-1c5a92843977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183618} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea841b68-bcb9-512c-a197-224a3b1f9a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c0fa171-9c01-5949-8df7-ed01d22c88ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-79194" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/a41a721eab2eb97e8b7b30ddf877e37a48d2b74eeef82ab0a4b72f3bb4a93603.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/f08846ce1f79cd14c593fbf37360fb8169a26704c956506ca0deedb8025e3b39.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$"}]}
